        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Dumbbells</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these dumbbells. They were chosen from published specifications,
            manufacturer product pages and manuals, warranty terms, recall records and independent
            coverage, and the claims on this page are limited to what those sources support.
          </p>
          <p>
            We started from mechanisms rather than brands, because the mechanism decides most of what
            owning a set is like. For each — pin selector, switch selector, twist handle, dial,
            plate-loaded screw lock and classic spin-lock — we looked for a set that the maker lists
            as on sale on 16 October 2026 and that at least one independent source had described in
            enough detail to cross-check. The independent sources were Garage Gym Reviews'
            specification tables, Science for Sport's adjustable dumbbell comparison, a detailed
            Ironmaster review on Garage-Gyms.com, Rogue Fitness's listing for the NÜOBELL, Iron
            Company's listing for the CAP set, and Wellworthy's write-up of the Nike set.
          </p>
          <p>
            We then checked each figure in two places. Weight ranges, step sizes, lengths and
            warranties went onto the cards only where both agreed. Handle lengths were the most
            common casualty: several makers publish one, but we could rarely find an independent
            figure that matched, so most cards leave it out. PowerBlock's smallest step is the other
            exception, explained on its card.
          </p>
          <p>
            Every brand was checked against the CPSC recall database, by maker name and in recall descriptions, along with the product type. That check removed the best-known
            adjustable dumbbell of all. The BowFlex SelectTech 552 and 1090 were recalled in June 2025
            — about 3.8 million units — because the plates can dislodge from the handle during use;
            the recall notice lists 337 reports of dislodging plates to the original seller, 111 of
            them with injuries, and tells owners to stop using them. BowFlex now sells
            "Results Series" SelectTech dumbbells that it says use metal components to attach the
            plates; the recall notice does not mention them either way, the line has little ownership
            history yet, and it shares the recalled design's name, so we have left it off this list. We also excluded the Amazon Basics 55 lb
            adjustable dumbbells (recalled May 2026), the FitRx SmartBell Quick-Select and SmartBell XL
            sets (recalled 2025 and 2026), the ProForm Rapid Strike 50 lb set and the NordicTrack 50 lb
            iSelect (both recalled 2024). Every one of those recalls describes the same failure: plates
            coming away from the handle.
          </p>
          <p>
            PowerBlock has one recall on record: in October 2024 it recalled the 5 lb grip handles
            supplied with its Commercial Pro 100 set, because plates above 55 lb could slip off them.
            The notice covers only those handles, not the Pro 100 EXP carded here, and we found no
            recalls for REP's dumbbells, Core Home Fitness, SMRTFT, Ironmaster, CAP Barbell or Nike's
            fitness equipment.
          </p>
          <p>
            Finally, we left out sets we could only verify from the maker. PowerBlock's Elite USA 90 is
            a well-regarded steel-plate alternative to the Pro 100 EXP, but we could not find an
            independent source that confirmed its figures on the day, so it is mentioned here rather
            than carded.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="recalls" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. What the Recalls Tell You About Mechanisms</h2>
<p>
            Five recalls in three years, across five brands, all describing plates that separate from
            the handle, is a pattern worth understanding before you buy any adjustable dumbbell.
          </p>
          <p>
            Most of the recalled sets share an architecture: the handle sits in a moulded tray, a dial,
            knob or rotating handle selects which plates are latched to it, and the latching is done
            by small internal parts. When those parts wear, crack or do not fully engage, a plate can
            stay attached while the dumbbell is in the tray and fall away once it is in the air. The
            Amazon Basics recall describes plates that "fail to engage completely during weight
            adjustment" — a failure the user cannot easily see.
          </p>
          <p>
            None of that means every dial or twist-handle dumbbell is unsafe; several on this list use
            twist or switch selectors and are not under any recall. It does mean three habits are worth
            adopting with any selector-type set:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Check engagement before every set.</strong> Lift the dumbbell a few inches
              out of its cradle, look at both ends, and set it back if anything looks uneven or a plate
              has shifted.</li>
            <li><strong>Never lift directly over your face or feet until you have checked.</strong>
              Presses and flyes put the head and chest directly under the plates.</li>
            <li><strong>Register the product and keep the serial number.</strong> Recalls are run by
              serial range, and makers contact registered owners first.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you are looking at a used set, the check in the next section applies — recalled sets
            still circulate on marketplaces.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="check-recall" class="scroll-mt-24">
