            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            <strong>Where to check.</strong> Search the product name on cpsc.gov or saferproducts.gov,
            open the recall notice, and compare the model, serial or lot number on your set against
            it. Some recalls cover only certain serial or lot ranges, so the number matters more than
            the name. If yours matches, stop using it and follow the remedy in the notice — the
            BowFlex, FitRx, ProForm, NordicTrack, PowerBlock and Amazon recalls all instruct owners to
            stop using the affected dumbbells or handles and offer a refund, repair or replacement.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>On newer BowFlex models.</strong> The June 2025 notice covers the 552 and 1090 by
            serial range. It does not mention the Results Series dumbbells BowFlex now sells, so the
            notice alone cannot tell you whether a Results Series set is affected; check with BowFlex
            using the serial number before relying on one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>For a used set with no visible numbers</strong>, do not buy it. A tray with the
            sticker removed, or a seller who cannot show you the label, is a reason to walk away.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pros-and-cons" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Adjustable Dumbbells: The Honest Pros and Cons</h2>
<p>
            The marketing for adjustable dumbbells is mostly about the pros. Here is the full list,
            weighted the way it tends to matter in a real home gym.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Pros</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Space.</strong> A pair and its cradles fit in a corner or under a bench. A
              fixed set covering the same range needs a rack several feet long.</li>
            <li><strong>Cost per range.</strong> Above about 40 lb a hand, an adjustable pair almost
              always costs less than buying every fixed pair it replaces.</li>
            <li><strong>Small, regular progression.</strong> Most sets move in 5 lb steps, and several
              offer 2.5 lb steps — useful for shoulders and arms, where a 5 lb jump is a large
              percentage.</li>
            <li><strong>Expandability.</strong> PowerBlock, REP and Ironmaster sell add-on plates,
              so you can buy a lighter set now and pay for heavier plates when you need them.</li>
            <li><strong>Tidiness.</strong> No loose dumbbells rolling around a spare room, which
              matters when children or pets share the space.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Cons</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Durability when dropped.</strong> Most selector and twist designs contain
              plastic or small internal parts, and several makers exclude drop damage from their
              warranties. This is the single biggest difference from fixed dumbbells.</li>
            <li><strong>Mechanism risk.</strong> As the recalls show, a selector mechanism can fail in
              a way a fixed dumbbell cannot.</li>
            <li><strong>Length and shape.</strong> Some designs stay full length even at their
              lightest setting, and every design is long at its maximum. Long dumbbells hit your
              shoulders on presses and your thighs on curls.</li>
            <li><strong>Change time.</strong> Seconds per change is fast for one person, but a superset
              between two weights, or two people training together, means constant adjusting.</li>
            <li><strong>One weight at a time.</strong> You cannot hold a heavy pair for rows and a light
              pair for raises at the same moment, which rules out some circuit and drop-set styles
              unless you own two sets.</li>
            <li><strong>Resale and repair.</strong> A fixed dumbbell lasts decades and resells easily.
              An adjustable set depends on the maker for spare parts, and a recalled model is hard to
              sell.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Read those lists together and the pattern is clear: adjustable dumbbells are a space and
            budget decision. If you have neither constraint, fixed dumbbells are simpler and tougher.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="adjustable-vs-fixed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Adjustable vs Fixed Dumbbells</h2>
<p>
            This is the question behind most searches for adjustable dumbbells, including the many
            that name Nike. Four factors decide it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Cost per pound</h3>
          <p>
            Work out cost per pound of total capacity: the price divided by the combined weight of the
            pair at its maximum. On the prices we saw on 16 October, the CAP spin-lock set works out
            at roughly $1.25 per pound, the Core set at about $3.80, the Ironmaster 75 lb set and the
            60 lb QuickDraw at under $5, the NÜOBELL at a little over $5, the PowerBlock Pro 100 EXP
            at about $6 and the Nike pair at nearly $7.
          </p>
          <p>
            That looks expensive until you count what a fixed set has to contain. Covering 10 to 65 lb
            in 5 lb steps, as the Nike adjustable pair does, takes twelve fixed pairs and about 900 lb
            of iron, plus a rack to hold it. Nike's own store illustrates the gap: on 16 October it
            listed the adjustable pair at $899, while its fixed dumbbell rack set covering only 5 to
            50 lb was $1,850. Fixed dumbbells win on cost only when you need a handful of weights — a
            light pair and a heavy pair for a specific programme — rather than the full range.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Space</h3>
          <p>
            An adjustable pair in its cradles takes about the floor area of a pair of shoeboxes side by
            side; on a stand it is a footprint of roughly two by three feet. A fixed set over the same
            range needs a two- or three-tier rack several feet long. In a spare room or a corner next to
            a treadmill — the situation our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-small-spaces/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">small-space treadmill guide</a>
            deals with — that difference often decides it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Speed of changes</h3>
          <p>
            Fixed dumbbells change in the time it takes to put one pair down and pick up another.
            Selector and twist designs take a few seconds per change if the dumbbell is already in its
            cradle. Plate-loaded screw-lock designs like the Ironmaster take longer, and spin-lock sets
            take longest of all. For straight sets with a rest between them, any of these is fine. For
            supersets, circuits and drop sets, the gap matters, and only the QuickDraw is described by
            its maker as changeable outside the cradle.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Durability when dropped</h3>
          <p>
            Fixed rubber or urethane dumbbells are built to be set down hard and occasionally dropped.
            Most adjustable designs are not. Our rule is simple: <strong>never drop a dial,
            twist-handle or selector dumbbell unless the maker explicitly says you may</strong>, and
            even then lower it under control whenever you can. Of the makers on this page, Ironmaster
            says outright that its all-metal Quick-Lock dumbbells can be dropped; REP says the
            QuickDraw is built to withstand occasional drops but recommends dropping flat and excludes
            damage from repeated drops from its warranty; Science for Sport reports that NÜOBELL's
            warranty does not cover drops and advises against dropping the Core set. PowerBlock and
            Nike do not address it on their product pages, so treat both as do-not-drop.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">So which?</h3>
          <p>
            Choose adjustable if you need more than four or five pairs, have limited floor space, or
            want to progress in small steps without buying new dumbbells. Choose fixed if you train
            with a partner, do a lot of circuit or drop-set work, tend to drop weights at the end of
            a set, or only need a few weights. Plenty of home gyms end up with both: an adjustable
            pair for the full range and one or two fixed pairs for the movements done most often.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mechanisms" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Which Mechanism Suits Which Training</h2>
<p>
            Every adjustable dumbbell on sale uses one of five mechanisms. Each suits some training
            better than others.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Pin selector (PowerBlock)</h3>
          <p>
            The handle sits inside a nest of plates and a pin selects how many come with it. Pin sets
            change quickly, go very heavy, and have few moving parts, which is why PowerBlock sells
            commercial versions. The cost is shape: a rectangular cage around the hand that some
            people find awkward for presses and curls. <strong>Best for</strong> heavy pressing,
            rowing and lunges, and for lifters who expect to outgrow a 50 lb set.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Switch selector (REP QuickDraw)</h3>
          <p>
            Steel switches on the handle lock plates on or off. It is the closest thing to a pin
            design with a traditional dumbbell shape, and REP describes it as drop-tested. The
            maximum is 60 lb. <strong>Best for</strong> general strength work, supersets and anyone
            who wants a classic dumbbell feel without a cage.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Twist handle (Core Home Fitness, NÜOBELL)</h3>
          <p>
            Rotate the handle in its cradle to select the weight. Twist designs have open handles and
            look and balance most like a fixed dumbbell. The mechanism is internal and usually
            includes plastic parts, which is the source of the durability complaints reviewers make.
            <strong>Best for</strong> controlled, moderate-weight training in a tidy space, where
            dumbbells are put back rather than dropped.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Dial (BowFlex-style)</h3>
          <p>
            Turn a dial at each end while the dumbbell sits in its tray. Dial sets are fast and
            familiar, and also the design most represented in recent recalls. Many are long and
            awkward at low weights because the full-size end plates stay on the handle.
            <strong>Best for</strong> people who change weight often in straight sets and will check
            engagement every time. We have not carded a dial set this year, for the reasons in
            <a href="#recalls" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">the recalls section</a>. Nike does
            not say on its product page how its selector works, so we have not placed it here.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Plate-loaded and spin-lock (Ironmaster, CAP)</h3>
          <p>
            Plates slide onto a handle and are held by a collar or locking screw. Nothing can
            disengage on its own the way a selector can, and an all-metal design is as tough as a
            fixed dumbbell — the reason Ironmaster tells owners they may drop theirs. The cost is
            change time and loose plates to store. Spin-lock collars, the oldest version, are cheap
            but slow and can work loose, so they need checking every set; a 2021 recall of one brand's
            Olympic dumbbell handles involved locking collars that slipped. <strong>Best for</strong>
            tight budgets (spin-lock), and for people who want an adjustable set that will survive
            heavy, unpolished use (Ironmaster).
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reading-listings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. How to Read an Adjustable Dumbbell Listing</h2>
<p>
            Five figures matter. Here is how to read each one, and what to check that the listing
            usually does not tell you.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Weight range per hand.</strong> Listings sometimes give the pair total — "110 lb
            set" — and sometimes the per-hand maximum. Make sure you know which. Then think about your
            heaviest movement, usually rows, goblet squats or lunges, not curls. A set that stops at
            50 lb is outgrown quickly by many people who train their legs with dumbbells.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Increments.</strong> A 5 lb step is fine for big movements and a large jump for
            lateral raises at 15 lb. If you train shoulders and arms seriously, 2.5 lb steps help.
            Check whether the smaller step needs an add-on plate that leaves the dumbbell slightly
            unbalanced, as REP describes for the QuickDraw.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Length at maximum.</strong> This is the figure most often missing from listings and
            the one that most affects comfort. On this page, maximum lengths run from about 14.5 inches
            (Core, Ironmaster) to about 19 inches (PowerBlock Pro 100 EXP). A long dumbbell knocks your
            shoulders at the bottom of a press and your thighs on a hammer curl. Fixed dumbbells of the
            same weight are usually shorter.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Handle diameter and length.</strong> Thicker handles are harder to hold on heavy
            rows and deadlift variations; the ones we could confirm on this page are roughly 32 to 35 mm. Handle length — the space
            between the plates — decides whether larger hands fit comfortably, and it is the figure
            we found hardest to cross-check, so measure your palm width and ask the seller if it
            matters to you.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Warranty and what it excludes.</strong> Read the exclusions, not just the term.
            A lifetime warranty that excludes drop damage, or a five-year warranty that is residential
            only, is still useful, but you should know before the first drop.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two further checks: whether the price is for a pair or a single dumbbell (PowerBlock notes
            its Amazon listing is a single), and whether a stand is included. Lifting a heavy dumbbell
            out of a cradle on the floor is a deadlift every time; most people end up buying the stand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safe-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Using Adjustable Dumbbells Safely at Home</h2>
<p>
            Most home dumbbell injuries are dropped weights and strained backs, not anything exotic.
            A few habits prevent most of them.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Treat ratings as maker figures.</strong> A maximum weight is what the maker
              designed the dumbbell to carry, not a test result from us and not a target. Stay within
              it, and never load extra plates beyond what the maker sells for that handle.</li>
            <li><strong>Check engagement every set.</strong> On selector and twist designs, look
              at both ends before the weight goes overhead or over your face. On spin-lock and
              screw-lock designs, check the collars or screws are tight.</li>
            <li><strong>Use the stand, and lift with your legs.</strong> Picking a 60 lb dumbbell out
              of a floor cradle with a rounded back is the most common way to strain something before
              the workout starts.</li>
            <li><strong>Lower, do not drop</strong>, unless the maker says otherwise — see
              <a href="#adjustable-vs-fixed" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">durability when dropped</a>.
              On a bench, bring the dumbbells to your thighs before sitting up rather than letting them
              fall to the sides.</li>
            <li><strong>Protect the floor.</strong> A rubber mat or tile under the training area stops
              a set-down from cracking tile or denting wood. Our guide to
              <a href="/best-home-gym-accessories/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home gym accessories worth buying</a>
              covers flooring and benches.</li>
            <li><strong>Think about the floor upstairs.</strong> A pair of dumbbells is not heavy for a
              domestic floor, but a stand, plates, a bench and a lifter concentrated in one corner add
              up. That is general guidance only; if the structure is unusual or you plan to add a rack,
              ask a qualified professional.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep children away from cradles.</strong> Selector mechanisms, loose plates and
              spin-lock collars are pinch and crush points.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you combine dumbbell work with running or walking, our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-before-or-after-weights/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">doing the treadmill before or after weights</a>
            covers the order of a combined session.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If you only need two or three weights</strong>, buy fixed dumbbells. A light pair
            for raises and a medium pair for rows and presses cost less than any set on this page and
            will outlast all of them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If space, budget or a downstairs neighbor is the real constraint</strong>, start with
            bands. A tube set with handles and a door anchor covers most upper-body work for a fraction of
            the price and fits in a drawer; our guide to the
            <a href="/best-resistance-bands/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best resistance bands</a>
            explains which type to buy and how they compare with dumbbells.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you drop weights at the end of hard sets</strong> and are not going to change
            that habit, buy fixed dumbbells or the Ironmaster, and nothing with a selector.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you already lift heavy with a barbell</strong>, a squat rack and plates may be a
            better use of the money; dumbbells then become the accessory rather than the main tool.
            Our guide to
            <a href="/best-squat-racks-for-home-gym/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">squat racks for a home gym</a>
            covers that route, and for pressing without a spotter, our
            <a href="/best-converging-chest-press-machines/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">converging chest press guide</a>
            looks at machines that let you push hard without weights over your face.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If your main goal is cardiovascular fitness</strong>, dumbbells are a complement,
            not a substitute. Our
            <a href="/home-cardio-equipment-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home cardio equipment guide</a>
            covers the machines.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you train with a partner who uses different weights</strong>, one adjustable pair
            will cause queues. Two cheaper sets, or fixed dumbbells for the most common weights, work
            better.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are adjustable dumbbells worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most home gyms, yes, if you need more than four or five weights and have limited space. A good pair replaces a full rack for less money and floor area. They are not worth it if you only need two or three weights, train with a partner on different loads, or tend to drop dumbbells, because fixed dumbbells are simpler and tougher.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are the pros and cons of adjustable dumbbells?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The pros are space, lower cost across a full range, small weight steps and the option to expand later. The cons are durability when dropped, the risk of a selector failing, a longer and sometimes awkward shape, time spent changing weight, and being able to use only one weight at a time. They are mainly a space and budget decision.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Nike make adjustable dumbbells?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. The Nike Strength store, run under licence by Dimension 6 Fitness, sells Nike Adjustable Dumbbells as a pair adjusting from 10 to 65 lb per hand in 5 lb steps, with cast-iron plates. On 16 October 2026 they were $899 a pair, or more with the stand. Nike does not describe the selector mechanism or a warranty on the product page.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are adjustable dumbbells better than fixed dumbbells?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither is better overall. Adjustable dumbbells win on space and on cost when you need a wide range. Fixed dumbbells win on durability, speed between weights, handle feel and simplicity, and on cost if you only need a few pairs. Many home gyms use an adjustable pair for the range plus one or two fixed pairs used most often.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you drop adjustable dumbbells?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. Most dial, twist-handle and selector designs contain plastic or small internal parts, and several makers exclude drop damage from the warranty. Only drop a set if the maker explicitly allows it; Ironmaster says its all-metal Quick-Lock can be dropped, and REP says the QuickDraw tolerates occasional flat drops. Otherwise, lower them under control.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What weight adjustable dumbbells should I buy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Choose by your heaviest likely movement, usually rows, goblet squats or lunges, not curls. Many beginners are well served by a set reaching 50 lb per hand; people who train their legs with dumbbells or already lift regularly often outgrow that and do better with 60 to 80 lb, or an expandable set that can be upgraded later.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying Adjustable Dumbbells</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these is easy to avoid before you order and expensive to fix afterwards.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a used set without checking the recall</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Millions of recalled dial-type dumbbells were sold, and plenty are resold on marketplaces. Before buying any used adjustable set, find the model and serial number, usually on the base or tray, and check it against the CPSC recall list. If the seller cannot show you the serial, walk away; a cheap set with a recalled mechanism is not a bargain.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing the maximum by your curl</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A 30 lb set feels plenty in the shop because people imagine curls. Rows, goblet squats, lunges and presses need far more, and a set you outgrow in a few months has to be sold and replaced. Pick the maximum for your heaviest movement, or buy an expandable set and add plates later.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Dropping a selector-type dumbbell</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Letting a dial or twist dumbbell fall to the floor at the end of a set is the quickest way to crack an internal part and void the warranty. Lower it to your thighs, then to the floor, or back to the cradle. If you know you drop weights when tired, buy a fixed pair or the all-metal Ironmaster instead.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the engagement check</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Selector sets can look fully locked while sitting in the cradle and still have a plate that has not engaged. Lift the dumbbell a few inches, look at both ends, and only then move it over your face or feet. It takes two seconds per set and is the main defence against the failure every recent recall describes.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

